  <p class="text-lg">
    Sputter is a fully autonomous pancake-making robot designed and built in a 4-person team. 
    It handles the complete process — dispensing batter, cooking, flipping, and plating — 
    achieving 92% end-to-end consistency.
  </p>

  <h2>The Challenge</h2>
  <p>
    The goal was to build a robot that could autonomously produce pancakes from raw batter 
    to a plated finished product with no human intervention. This required solving problems 
    across mechanical design, fluid delivery, motion control, and heat management.
  </p>

  <h2>Pneumatic Batter Delivery</h2>
  <p>
    One of the trickiest problems was reliably dispensing viscous batter. We developed a pneumatic 
    pressure system using a custom-designed 2L bottle cap with O-ring sealing and a regulated 
    20 psi air supply. This gave us consistent, controllable batter flow without the issues 
    of peristaltic or gravity-fed systems.
  </p>

  <h2>Mechanical Design</h2>
  <p>
    The robot is built on a fully custom PLA frame with a 3-axis Cartesian gantry, all modeled 
    in SolidWorks and 3D printed. The design prioritized rigidity, modularity, and rapid iteration — 
    we went through several frame revisions during development.
  </p>
  <p>
    A Geneva mechanism handles the cooking station rotation, providing precise indexing between 
    the dispensing, cooking, flipping, and plating positions.
  </p>

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>92% consistency rate</strong> across multiple test runs</li>
    <li>Fully autonomous operation from batter to plate</li>
    <li>Custom pneumatic, mechanical, and control systems</li>
    <li>Designed and fabricated in under 4 weeks</li>
  </ul>

